short-code {"wall_s": 3.4940868750000003, "ttft_s": 0.3981157500000001, "text": "Here is a complete implementation of an LRU (Least Recently Used) Cache in Python. It uses a `dict` for $O(1)$ lookups and a `DoublyLinkedList` to maintain the order of usage, ensuring $O(1)$ insertion, deletion, and access.\n\n### Implementation\n\n```python\nimport unittest\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n# --- Doubly Linked List Node ---\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, key=None, value=None):\n        self.key = key\n        self.value = value\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\n# --- Doubly Linked List ---\nclass DoublyLinkedList:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # Dummy head and tail to simplify edge cases\n        self.head = Node()\n        self.tail = Node()\n        self.head.next = self.tail\n        self.tail.prev = self.head\n        self.size = 0\n\n    def add_to_front(self, node):\n        \"\"\"Add a node right after the head (most recently used).\"\"\"\n        node.prev = self.head\n        node.next = self.head.next\n        self.head.next.prev = node\n        self.head.next = node\n        self.size +=", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 83, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 339, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 83}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-code", "thinking": false}
short-story {"wall_s": 3.2332582500000004, "ttft_s": 0.14940816699999981, "text": "The brass bell of the chronometer didn\u2019t tick; it hummed, a low, vibrating thrum that Elias felt in his molars. He wiped the salt spray from the lens of the Great Light, his rag leaving streaks in the condensation. Outside, the Atlantic was a churning black void, but inside the lantern room, the air smelled of ozone and old paper.\n\nOn the pedestal, where the logbook should have been, sat a single sheet of paper. It wasn\u2019t wet. It wasn\u2019t damp. It was dry, crisp, and smelled faintly of burnt toast.\n\nElias picked it up. The handwriting was his own, but jagged, hurried, as if written by a hand shaking from cold or terror.\n\n*Don\u2019t light the lamp at 03:00. The fog isn\u2019t fog. It\u2019s them. If you light it, they find the island. Burn the wick. Hide in the cellar. Wait for the tide to turn. \u2013 E.*\n\nElias stared at the clock. The second hand swept past the twelve. 02:58.\n\n\"Ridiculous,\" he muttered, though his voice lacked conviction. He was a man of routine, of oil", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 84, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 340, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 84}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-story", "thinking": false}
short-reasoning {"wall_s": 3.2433099999999992, "ttft_s": 0.16262037499999948, "text": "To determine the number of workers needed, we must first calculate the effective capacity available for standard operations after reserving space for spikes, and then divide the total incoming requests by that effective capacity.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate Effective Capacity per Worker\n\nEach worker has a raw capacity of 20 requests per second. However, 25% of this capacity must be reserved for spikes. This means only 75% of the worker's capacity is available for the steady-state load.\n\n$$\n\\text{Effective Capacity per Worker} = \\text{Raw Capacity} \\times (1 - \\text{Spike Reserve})\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Effective Capacity per Worker} = 20 \\times (1 - 0.25)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Effective Capacity per Worker} = 20 \\times 0.75 = 15 \\text{ requests per second}\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Number of Workers Needed\n\nThe service receives 120 requests per second. We divide the total load by the effective capacity of a single worker to find the number of workers required.\n\n$$\n\\text{Workers Needed} = \\frac{\\text{Total Requests per", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 101, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 357, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 101}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-reasoning", "thinking": false}
